{"reference":"Matthew 7","book":"Matthew","chapter":7,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Reading the Golden Rule","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Matthew 7:12 states a reciprocal command: act toward others as you would want them to act toward you. It cannot legitimately be used to excuse abuse, stigmatize disability or mental illness, enforce misogyny or anti-LGBTQ harm, blame poverty, set apocalyptic dates, or map ancient judgments onto modern peoples.","verse_refs":["Matthew 7:12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_007_d4a1cf0af9c8a12349c52968"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The Ending Formula","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The statement that Jesus had “finished these words” at 7:28 belongs to a repeated formula that marks the ends of large teaching collections in Matthew. Five such endings organize these collections, though readers differ over whether they establish a single symbolic outline for the book.","verse_refs":["Matthew 7:28"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_matthew_007_31a115bcd166cd423dbc0506"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}